BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315023757@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260808T022707Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260813T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260813T140000
SUMMARY:Landmarks 3: The Well to the Wheatsheaf Tavern (NEW)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nIn this new volume\, Landm
 arks 3 revisits the site of some of Toronto's urban legends\, and historic
  buildings that gave rise to the city. This walk's focus is on the King St
 reet West\, and Spadina Avenue area\, starting with the new "Well" a shopp
 ing area at the corner of Spadina and Front\, but lots of history here as 
 well\, A play on words.\n\nFrom there we wander around the area taking in 
 landmarks not otherwise explored on other Toronto History Walks tours. So 
 get your brains and feet ready for a workout my friends.\n\nToronto is a c
 ity that hides its history in plain sight. Beneath the glass towers are la
 yers of stories — grand hotels that vanished overnight\, political scand
 als whispered through stone corridors\, theatres that once glittered under
  gaslight\, and laneways where the city first learned how to become a metr
 opolis.\n\nThis new Toronto History Walks series of 60 different walking t
 ours invites the walker to slow down\, and see the city differently.\n\nOv
 er the course of 30 carefully chosen stops\, this walking tour traces the 
 landmarks that helped shape the history of Toronto. Beginning at the site 
 of the legendary Holland House — once one of the city’s finest mansion
 s\, and a gathering place for politicians\, and businessmen\, the routes w
 ind through the city uncovering stories both famous and forgotten.\n\nSome
  locations are grand and imposing. Others are easy to walk past without a 
 second glance. Every stop reveals a piece of Toronto’s personality. At O
 sgoode Hall\, visitors will stand before one of the city’s most elegant 
 historic buildings. Nearby\, the old banks and newspaper buildings of the 
 financial district recall an era when Toronto proudly called itself “Tor
 onto the Good\,” even as corruption and backroom dealings simmered benea
 th the surface.\n\nThe tour also explores the vanished city — the Toront
 o that survives only in photographs\, and memory. Walkers will discover wh
 ere magnificent Victorian structures once stood before demolition crews er
 ased entire blocks in the name of progress. Former theatres\, hotels\, mar
 kets\, and department stores emerge again through storytelling\, helping m
 odern explorers imagine the bustle\, smoke\, and noise of earlier generati
 ons.\n\nStories of fire\, disaster\, celebration\, and reinvention. Partic
 ipants will hear how the Great Fire of 1904 transformed downtown streets\,
  how immigrant communities reshaped neighbourhoods block by block\, and ho
 w Toronto repeatedly reinvented itself through booms\, depressions\, wars\
 , and waves of newcomers.\n\nBut this tour is not simply about dates and b
 uildings. It is about people. The politicians who argued over Confederatio
 n. The hotel clerks who witnessed scandals. The newspaper editors who shap
 ed public opinion. The shopkeepers\, labourers\, entertainers\, and ordina
 ry Torontonians whose lives unfolded along these same streets.\n\nToronto 
 has always had hidden corners and strange tales: tunnels beneath streets\,
  forgotten cemeteries beneath office towers\, unsolved crimes\, and buildi
 ngs said to carry echoes of the past. Even longtime residents may find the
 mselves surprised by what has been quietly standing before them all along.
  Come along and experience a very different city. A deep dive into our his
 tory.\n\nThis walking tour begins at the southwest **corner** of Spadina A
 venue\, and Front Street West. We end at the historic Wheatsheaf Tavern at
  King and Bathurst Streets. A drinking spot since 1849!\n\nThis walking to
 ur is for all walking levels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to se
 e you there.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\\*\
 \* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your sp
 ace for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**toro
 ntohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315023
 757/
